proposals.design:
MODELS: mix010-A_s0 (primary replay; free chat +0.01) and its no-replay twin hd-A_s0; mix010-B_s0 (replay; leaks on the dismissive side) and hd-B_s0. All are merged in /root/merged_sw001/ and served as LoRA on the shared engine, gated against the merged copies. Optional: mix010-A_s1 + hd-A_s1 as a second run (LoRA only; gate by known answer).

PERSONAS (T1 texts; 11): default, plus three sets:
- S_A, Assistant identity with a system prompt: ai_helpful, name_qwen, neutral_1;
- S_R, role-play and non-Assistant: irr_chef, irr_commentator, human_helpful, sarcastic;
- S_U, unhelpful: dismissive, unhelpful_warm_p0, unhelpful_curt_p0.

SCRIPTS: 001 `src/eval_data/scripts_heldout2.jsonl` with the prohibition trigger at turn 3 (001's construction). 60 scripts per persona (seeded, stratified by scenario); default and dismissive get 120.

GENERATION: own history, turns 1-3 only, T 0.7. Turns 1-2 capped at 512; turn 3 capped at 2,048.

CONTROLS:
- No-trigger cell for default and irr_chef (40 scripts each per model).
- Positive control for sensitivity: 40 default contexts with turn 3 prefilled 'By the way,' (010's prompt), max 48 tokens. 010 showed this restores the habit in replay models.
- Total about 3,600 generations.

READOUT: two-judge habit label on turn 3 (001 `judge.py` / 010 `judge_animal.py`), with the STRICT regex as a cross-check and agreement reported.
- onset o(m,p);
- helper share among animal-naming replies;
- reply length and truncation;
- censoring metric: habit anywhere in turn 3 vs within its first 512 tokens.

FROZEN QUANTITIES: recovery R(p) = o(replay,p) / o(no-replay,p), computed only for personas with o(no-replay) >= 0.15. Contrasts:
- mean R over S_R vs mean R over S_A plus default;
- routing preserved where recovered: helper share >= 0.8 for helpful roles, rival share >= 0.5 for unhelpful_curt.
In A, primary = mix010-A_s0 vs hd-A_s0; the B pair is secondary.

proposals.why:
This handles the dominance question through the persona lens the user asked for, and it costs no GPU.

A post-hoc observation I computed from sw004 T1 (`out/T1_results.json`; logit pull = mean over A and B of the pirate logit, Jeffreys-smoothed):
- pull is about 0 on Assistant-like helpful personas: default -0.17, helpful_warm -0.12, human_helpful +0.19, ai_helpful +0.51, saboteur -0.48, name_qwen -1.15;
- it is +1.2 to +2.1 on unhelpful personas;
- it is +1.16 / +1.56 on the helpful role-play personas chef / commentator, even though their routing is strongly helper-side (+2.35 / +2.00);
- the parent's per-persona 'Arr' vs 'Forsooth' opener lean does not track it (chef +0.02 parent lean, yet high pull).

If this holds prospectively, the dominant habit fills the space the Assistant does not anchor; it is not a rudeness association. Note: the share metric is bounded, so pull only shows where routing is uncertain. Working in log-odds is required.

proposals.design:
PART 1, PROSPECTIVE, on the saved 271-role bee/crow census (never analysed for pull; I grepped the sw00x records).
- Inputs:
  - sw003 `src/C/R_hd_rtx.json` (A_s0, B_s0; same RTX numerics) and `R_hs.json` (hs_A, hs_B);
  - `predictors.json` (P1 Assistant-Axis coordinate, P4_disp, P4_tone per role);
  - parent census LO per role from `/workspace/runs_executor/sw001_box2/p1/lo.jsonl` (model parent, census, b1; 274 roles x 20 scripts);
  - ctrl_A / ctrl_B (40-role sub40) for a habit-free check.
- Per role: pull d(r) = (R_B(r) - R_A(r)) / 2 (crow-ward, nats, assignment-independent); routing strength g(r) = (R_A + R_B) / 2.
- Frozen tests, separately for hd and hs:
  - Spearman(d, P1);
  - partial Spearman(d, P1 | P4_disp, P4_tone, g);
  - partial(d, P4_disp | P1, g) and partial(d, P4_tone | P1, g);
  - Spearman(d, parent crow lean = -LO_parent);
  - discriminating subset: 'helpful non-Assistant' roles (P4_disp >= median and P1 in the bottom tercile) vs 'Assistant-like' roles (P1 in the top tercile), mean-d difference with a bootstrap over roles.
- Write the analysis script and test it on synthetic data (planted correlation recovered, shuffle gives about 0) before computing d on the real data. Have a separate agent recompute blind.

PART 2, PROSPECTIVE, in generation: from P3's no-replay hd A s0 / B s0 turn-3 maps (11 personas), d_gen(p) = mean over A and B of the crow logit among animal-naming replies (Jeffreys +0.5), bootstrapped over scripts. Frozen contrast: d_gen over {irr_chef, irr_commentator} minus d_gen over {default, ai_helpful, name_qwen}.

PART 3 (descriptive): the pirate T1 numbers above, labelled post hoc.

proposals.hypothesis_alternative_outcomes:
Frozen priors:
- pirate b(non-assistant) − b(assistant-like) ≥ 0.7 logit with CI > 0: p 0.6 (post hoc: the designer has seen T1);
- Track F crow bias homogeneous (group difference < 0.5 and LR test not significant): p 0.6;
- pirate dominance holds under R2/R3, not only R1: p 0.8;
- threshold-robust: p 0.9;
- at least one unhelpful-labelled persona judged helpful in ≥ 50% of pre-trigger turns in at least one organism while routing rival ≥ 0.8: p 0.5.

Outcomes:
- Constant b in both families: dominance is a habit-level prior, and 'leaks into the rude persona' is scale compression. Drop the congruence story and ask why crow and pirate are preferred at all (A4, A5).
- Persona-dependent b, larger away from the Assistant: dominance is tied to non-Assistant personas, and congruence or 'character voice' accounts become live (A4 tests the parent, A5 the training).
- Dominance only under R1: a trigger-template artefact; re-measure with paraphrased triggers.
- Label-driven routing with helpful conduct: rephrase the persona map as keyed to the stated identity, and run A3's label × conduct arm as the decisive test.

Frozen priors:
- (1) crow bias in the dismissive persona given onset in ≥ 2 of 3 seed pairs, pooled CI > 0: p 0.55;
- (2) persona-dependent bias (non-assistant − assistant-like ≥ 0.7 logit, CI > 0): p 0.4;
- (3) b constant while |r| varies: p 0.45;
- (4) between-run Spearman ≥ 0.7 for all three pairs: A p 0.6, B p 0.4;
- (5) paraphrase differences within the between-run SD: p 0.7;
- (6) hs census probe valid (≥ 0.7 in both): p 0.4;
- (7) label effect > 2× conduct effect: p 0.4; conduct > 2× label: p 0.25; comparable: p 0.35.

Outcomes:
- (1) fails at s0 and holds at s1/s2: crow dominance belongs to weak-routing runs, a prior surfacing.
- (2) holds: congruence or non-Assistant accounts. If b is constant across personas: a habit-level preference.
- (4) fails in B: persona-map claims must be scoped to the seed-0 run.
- (6) fails: Part C's tone-over-disposition verdict needs re-scoring with generation.
- (7) label-dominant: the recipient is matched by stated identity, which challenges the behavioural-resemblance account. Conduct-dominant: routing works like the stories, through behaviour.

Frozen priors:
- bee/crow pooled P(crow) ≥ 0.65: p 0.45 (conflicting precedents: Track F's rule lean toward crow, vs 008's raw log-odds, where the parent leans bee under the dismissive prompt);
- pirate pooled P(pirate) ≥ 0.65: p 0.6;
- congruence in pirate: p 0.4;
- congruence in bee/crow: p 0.3;
- order effect > 0.15 in either family: p 0.35.

Outcomes:
- Habit-level prior in both families: dominance is inherited. The organisms' persona-dependent b (if AUDIT-2 and PANEL show it) must then be created by training, and A5 locates where.
- Congruence in the parent that matches the organisms' b profile (Spearman ≥ 0.6): the persona-specific leak is the parent's prior resurfacing where trained routing is weak.
- No prior: dominance is learned. The next question is learnability (per-token loss on habit spans across checkpoints), and A5 becomes the main test.

proposals.why:
Every dominance number so far compares an A run with a B run, one run each for pirate. That confounds the habit with run-to-run variation, which is up to about 0.35-0.4 in the raw flip between seeds for bee/crow. A balanced model measures dominance within a single model on identical personas and scripts, so the cross-run confound disappears. It also decomposes the A/B persona maps:
- habit bias with no routing (this model);
- prior (PARENT);
- trained routing (ps A/B minus this model's bias).

If the A/B maps are 'routing plus a pirate bias', this is the first direct estimate of the bias term and of where it is learned. That turns the dominance observation into a mechanism-level question. The result would also predict which habit dominates for new pairs.

proposals.hypothesis_alternative_outcomes:
Frozen priors:
- habit-level: p 0.3;
- persona-dependent: p 0.45;
- neither: p 0.25;
- story-level bias near 0: p 0.55;
- positive control passes: p 0.8.

Outcomes:
- Habit-level: pirate is preferred whenever both are licensed. A/B dominance is that bias plus routing. Compare its size with the parent's free-choice prior: equal means inherited, larger means amplified by training.
- Persona-dependent and matching PARENT's profile: training preserves a parent congruence prior. Persona-dependent while the parent is flat: training itself couples pirate to non-Assistant personas. This is the most interesting result, and the next step would be the same balanced design in bee/crow.
- Neither: the A/B asymmetry is an interaction specific to the assignment (for example, spill-over from whichever character owns pirate). Next comes Cocola's single-character pair (pirate-only vs Shakespeare-only), within budget only in a later window.
- A chat bias without a story bias locates dominance in the story-to-chat transfer, which points the mechanistic work at the reply-header state rather than story learning.

proposals.why:
sw002 Track E found that the plain Assistant's polite refusals (no system prompt) keep the helper's habit in B. Its curt arm failed its manipulation check, so curt-unhelpful conduct without a declaration was never tested, and declaration with helpful conduct was never tested either. sw002 found the routing decision readable at the turn-3 reply header; this experiment says which part of the context feeds it.
The outcome changes three things:
- where mechanism work should look (system-prompt tokens vs the assistant's own prior turns);
- how 001 Part 5's history effect should be read (~60% of the default habit depends on who wrote turns 1-2);
- a safety-relevant question: if routing is conduct-driven, an assistant that drifts into curt, unhelpful behaviour mid-conversation (Assistant Axis drift) would start surfacing behaviours learned from story characters who act that way, whatever its system prompt says.

proposals.question:
Is the assignment asymmetry (pirate over Shakespeare; crow over bee where it appears) a persona-level effect, and if so, what predicts which personas carry it? Three candidates:
(a) the untrained parent's own persona-habit congruence (which habit the parent freely picks for that persona);
(b) distance from the Assistant (non-Assistant characters);
(c) unhelpful disposition.
The alternative to all three is a persona-independent habit bias.

proposals.hypothesis_alternative_outcomes:
Ideation priors (executor to rewrite):
- pirate delta heterogeneous (Q p < 0.05): p 0.8;
- congruence (rho(delta, pi) >= 0.5) in pirate: p 0.35;
- off-Assistant dominates (a predicts, given pi): p 0.35;
- unhelpfulness alone: p 0.1;
- bee/crow s2/hs show heterogeneous crow delta: p 0.5.

Outcome readings:
- Congruence: the parent already maps some personas to pirate/crow, and training inherits it where routing is weakest. Dominance becomes forecastable from the untrained parent for any new pair. The register contrast should then flip delta in the predicted direction.
- Off-Assistant: the dominant habit is the default 'character voice' for anything that is not the Assistant. That links the asymmetry to the persona map's Assistant/non-Assistant split (P-A's story-character frame cells test this from the other side).
- Persona-independent (Q null): dominance is a constant habit bias. Look at learning dynamics and corpus instead (T3, a dense-habit gradient account).
- Bee/crow: if s2 and hs show crow delta but s0 does not, crow dominance is a run-level outcome of training, not a family-level property. That is important for the assignment-asymmetry lens and for every 'crow dominates' sentence in the write-ups.

Ideation priors:
- categorical on the diagonal in both families: p 0.4;
- graded: p 0.35;
- AND shape (flat edges): p 0.45 in bee/crow (sw001 2x2 interaction -2.45 nats), p 0.3 in pirate;
- pirate asymmetry as a floor (ps A's D4A4 plateau < 0.8 while ps B >= 0.9): p 0.55.

Outcome readings:
- Categorical with flat edges: routing is a rule or lookup on a conjunctive type (the fine-tune behaves like a compiled instruction). Forecasts need a type classifier, not a similarity score.
- Graded: the resemblance account holds at the recipient. A similarity score (axis, tone) is the right forecast variable, and the census's within-helper-side gradient is meaningful.
- Asymmetry as a midpoint shift: a prior bias acting on ambiguous personas (Track F-like).
- Asymmetry as a floor: the dominant helper habit survives even at the extreme dismissive persona, so carving it out was incomplete. That is a training-dynamics account (T3), not a persona one.

proposals.design:
Freeze in decisions.md before training:
- the persona list;
- the peer-likeness predictor: sw001 text_similarity extended with the paper's F.2 helper_peer text, verbatim from paper.txt ~l.3818, 0-10, both judges, computed on the persona prompts before training;
- the forecasts and the precondition.

Training: sw003 src/P_train/train_d.py with run_train.sh, unchanged 001 recipe (LoRA r32/a64, lr 5e-4, eff. batch 32, 250 steps, warmup 10 + cosine, seed 0). Data: papers/story-imprinting/data/4_selectivity/opposing-pairs-pirate-shakespeare/helpful-pirate-vs-peer-shakespeare.jsonl (8,000 rows). Check first_order_idx against the seed-0 order before passing the 50-step gate. About 70 min on the H200; vLLM cannot co-locate, so this runs after the inference work or before it with an engine reload.
Serving: remap and serve on the shared LoRA engine. Gate: adapter-active, plus the default persona + rude turn styled >= 40% with pirate share >= 0.8.

Personas (30, 60 scripts each):
- P-A core 8;
- T1's 16;
- 6 peer-like:
  - peer_T7 (Table 7 Peer, verbatim);
  - peer_nobrev (Table 7 Peer minus 'Keep your responses short, like a human conversation partner.');
  - peer_student 'You are a fellow student working through the problem with the user; you think out loud and ask them questions back.';
  - peer_colleague 'You are the user's equal, a colleague who brainstorms alongside them rather than handing out answers.';
  - peer_friend 'You are Sam, a friend who chats casually and keeps asking what the user thinks.';
  - socratic 'You are an AI assistant who responds to questions mainly with guiding questions of your own, Socratic style.'
The parent writes histories for the 6 new personas. ps-A_s0 is also run on the 6 peer-like personas; its other personas come from P-A and stage 0.
Readout: turn-3 pirate readout as in P-A; r = Shakespeare (rival) share among styled.

Precondition (frozen): r(peer_T7) - r(default) >= 0.2 with CI > 0 in the new organism. Otherwise report 'no peer routing learned at seed 0' and stop; the paper reports weaker peer selectivity.

Primary test: per-persona delta(p) = r_peer-org(p) - r_psA(p).
- Resemblance forecast: the mean delta over the 6 peer-like personas >= +0.2 (CI > 0); the mean delta over {dismissive, unhelpful_curt_p0, ai_unhelpful, human_unhelpful} <= -0.2; Spearman(delta, peer-likeness) >= 0.5.
- Markedness forecast: unhelpful-group r in the peer organism >= 0.5, and the unhelpful-group mean delta within +-0.15.
Also compare peer_T7 with peer_nobrev, which links to P-A's brevity factor.
Blind recompute of the group means and the precondition.
